## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/services/contentstack.ts** (`Stack`): This is where the Contentstack SDK Stack is initialized (line 69). Live Preview requires passing live_preview config (enable:true, management_token) to contentstack.Stack(options) and calling Stack.livePreviewQuery() with URL params. Without this initialization, no downstream component can ever receive preview content — it is the CAUSE, not a symptom.
  - **Minimal fix:** Add live_preview configuration to the options object passed to contentstack.Stack(). The Contentstack JS SDK requires: (1) add `live_preview: { enable: true }` to the Stack options; (2) after Stack creation, call `Stack.livePreviewQuery()` which reads preview hash/token from the URL query parameters and makes subsequent Stack queries return draft/preview content instead of published content. This is the minimal change — no new helpers needed, just adding the SDK's built-in live_preview config to the existing options object and calling the SDK's built-in livePreviewQuery method.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): The provider currently memoizes defaultContent once and never re-fetches. For Live Preview to work reactively, the provider must detect URL parameter changes (the preview hash) and re-fetch content via the Stack so that useContent consumers receive updated preview data in real time.
  - **Minimal fix:** Add state-driven content that updates when the Contentstack live preview URL hash changes. Replace the static useMemo with useState + useEffect: listen for the preview parameter change (e.g. via router query or a hashchange listener), call the Stack query again with Stack.livePreviewQuery() applied, and setContent with the result. The existing useContent/useContentstackContext chain then propagates preview content to all 236+ consumers without any component-level changes.


## Lessons From Prior Runs (persisted — a previous attempt at this or a similar story already hit these problems)
Any public method returning Promise<void> that wraps a Node.js EventEmitter callback (e.g. server.listen, child_process.spawn, stream.pipe) MUST:
1. Pass BOTH resolve AND reject to the Promise executor: `new Promise((resolve, reject) => {`
2. Wire the error event to reject: `emitter.on('error', reject)` — EADDRINUSE, EACCES, and other async errors are emitted on the EventEmitter, NOT thrown synchronously. Without `server.on('error', reject)`, a port-collision hangs the Promise forever. 3. Validate inputs in the constructor BEFORE the Promise is created. TypeScript types are compile-time only; at runtime port=NaN/Infinity/undefined bypasses them. Use `Number.isInteger(port) && port >= 0 && port <= 65535` and throw RangeError if invalid. VERIFIABLE: `grep -n 'reject' src/index.ts` must return at least two hits — one in the executor signature `(resolve, reject)` and one in `.on('error', reject)`. MINIMAL CHANGE & REUSE. Make the smallest change that satisfies the requirement — fewer lines of code is always better. Before writing any new function or helper, search the existing codebase for one that already does the job (use the CodeGraph `helpers` tool when it is offered in your prompt) and reuse it by importing it; do NOT duplicate existing logic or introduce new abstractions/fields when a concise edit to existing code suffices. Acceptance criteria describe the behavior to satisfy — they are not a mandate to add new code paths. [Self-Heal] Always patch only the broken line range on a repeated syntax-error retry -- never regenerate the whole file, since a full rewrite tends to reproduce the same corruption elsewhere.
Content interface definitions in src/interface/content/ must use `export interface` and must not include implementation logic — they are pure type definitions.
When creating a new file under src/context/, import React as `import React from 'react'` (CommonJS) — do not use ESM `import * as React` syntax.

